<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xml:lang="en"><generator uri="https://jekyllrb.com/" version="4.4.1">Jekyll</generator><link href="https://6michelestingo9.github.io/feed.xml" rel="self" type="application/atom+xml"/><link href="https://6michelestingo9.github.io/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" hreflang="en"/><updated>2026-08-04T16:01:38+00:00</updated><id>https://6michelestingo9.github.io/feed.xml</id><title type="html">blank</title><subtitle></subtitle><entry><title type="html">Lost&amp;amp;Found - a misplaced manifesto</title><link href="https://6michelestingo9.github.io/blog/2026/misplaced-manifesto/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Lost&amp;amp;Found - a misplaced manifesto"/><published>2026-08-04T10:00:00+00:00</published><updated>2026-08-04T10:00:00+00:00</updated><id>https://6michelestingo9.github.io/blog/2026/misplaced-manifesto</id><content type="html" xml:base="https://6michelestingo9.github.io/blog/2026/misplaced-manifesto/"><![CDATA[<div class="row justify-content-center"> <div class="col-sm-7 mt-3 mt-md-0"> <figure> <picture> <source class="responsive-img-srcset" srcset="/assets/img/dore-astolfo-moon-480.webp 480w,/assets/img/dore-astolfo-moon-800.webp 800w,/assets/img/dore-astolfo-moon-1400.webp 1400w," type="image/webp" sizes="95vw"/> <img src="/assets/img/dore-astolfo-moon.jpg" class="img-fluid rounded z-depth-1" width="100%" height="auto" alt="Gustave Doré: a winged chariot arriving at an immense cratered moon, the earth a speck in the starfield below" data-zoomable="" loading="eager" onerror="this.onerror=null; $('.responsive-img-srcset').remove();"/> </picture> </figure> </div> </div> <div class="caption"> Gustave Doré, <em>In Elijah's chariot, St. John and Astolfo travel to the moon</em> — for Ariosto's <em>Orlando Furioso</em>, 1879. Engraved by Ch. Barbant. </div> <h2 id="what-this-is">What this is</h2> <p>A lost and found is where things end up when they don’t belong anywhere else, but are too good to throw away. A cable that fits nothing you own. A book someone left behind. Objects — or ideas — that lost their context and are waiting for it to come back.</p> <p>It is like the Ariostan lunar valley in <a href="/notes/stallings-lost-and-found/">A.E. Stallings - Lost and Found</a>, where everything misplaced on earth accrues, waiting for a frame that can make sense of it again — a frame we have been missing <a href="/notes/eco-search-for-the-perfect-language/">since Babel</a>, when name and thing stopped coinciding.</p> <p>No one has put it back together, and the search keeps shedding loose material; a lost and found is one of the places it drifts to.</p> <p>That is roughly what this place is for.</p> <h2 id="what-i-actually-work-on">What I actually work on</h2> <p>My work sits at the intersection of language models and knowledge representation: cognitive agents, multi-agent architectures, reasoning systems, and the question of how structured knowledge and neural models can be made to cooperate instead of merely coexist.</p> <p>The thread running through it is that language models are more interesting as <em>components</em> than as products: their meaning is in the <a href="/notes/wittgenstein-philosophical-investigations/">use</a>, not in the model. A model that generates fluent text is a solved problem.</p> <p>A system that remembers, coordinates, plans, revises its own conclusions, and can explain why it reached them — that is still mostly open. Getting there needs both the statistical machinery and the <a href="/notes/wittgenstein-tractatus/">symbolic scaffolding</a> — and an ear for what even that must “pass over in silence.” The interesting problems live in the seam between them.</p> <p>Expect notes from that seam: graphs and ontologies, reasoning that has to survive contact with an actual system, engineering write-ups from building agents that need to be reliable rather than impressive.</p> <h2 id="notes-for-the-curious-and-the-easily-distracted">Notes for the curious and the easily distracted</h2> <p>The subtitle is not self-deprecation. Distraction is a method.</p> <p>The useful idea is rarely the one you set out to find — it is usually adjacent to it, in some neighbouring field, in a footnote, in the tool you had to build to run the experiment. Following that adjacency is how you find the connections that a straight line through the problem would have missed. That is <a href="/notes/merton-barber-serendipity/">serendipity</a> in the strict sense — accident <em>and</em> sagacity, never one without the other. The cost is that you end up holding a lot of loose material with no obvious home.</p> <p>Hence the lost and found.</p> <h2 id="what-this-is-not">What this is not</h2> <p>Not a tutorial site. Not polished. Not on a schedule. Some entries will be a few paragraphs and a link; others will be long enough to have needed an editor they did not get. A few will turn out to be wrong, and I would rather leave them up with a correction than quietly delete them.</p> <p>If something here is useful to you, take it. That is what a lost and found is for.</p> <hr/> <h3 id="adjacencies">Adjacencies</h3> <p>What sits next to what. Same idea as above, only drawn.</p> <div class="lf-graph" id="lf-graph" data-baseurl="" data-sources="off"> <div class="lf-controls"> <label class="lf-ctl"><input type="checkbox" class="lf-toggle-sources"/> sources</label> <button type="button" class="lf-btn lf-fit">fit</button> <span class="lf-hint">scroll = zoom · drag background = pan · drag a node to move it</span> <a class="lf-full" href="/notes/map/">open full map ↗</a> </div> <div class="lf-legend"> <span><i style="background: #eab308"></i>manifesto</span> <span><i style="background: #3b82c4"></i>note</span> <span><i style="background: #9aa0a6"></i>source</span> </div> </div> <script type="application/json" id="lf-graph-data">
